// Companion LPC-level test for the "intended use" replace_program() demo
// (testsuite/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_*.lpc) -- the
// legitimate runtime-polymorphism counterpart to the redundant
// self-target no-op handled by replace_program_is_noop() (src/packages/
// core/replace_program.cc). See the shell fixture's header
// comment for the full pattern writeup, INCLUDING why the shell has to
// `inherit` all 3 concrete candidate types up front (replace_program()
// can only swap to something already inherited).
//
// WHAT THIS FILE CAN AND CANNOT CHECK:
//
// replace_programs() (the actual swap) only runs from the backend's
// gametick queue (src/vm/internal/vm.cc's remove_destructed_objects(),
// scheduled every 5 minutes by src/backend.cc, or one gametick after the
// reclaim_objects() efun) -- and this test harness's single top-level
// -ftest run never yields back to the real event loop mid-suite, so no
// gametick ever elapses between two test files' do_tests() calls (the
// same reason call_out.lpc only ever asserts call_out_info() metadata,
// never a callback's actual side effect, within one do_tests()). So what
// THIS test can honestly pin, immediately after clone_object(), is the
// PENDING state: the shell object still IS running the shell's own
// program (its own soon-to-be-shed function is still there, and it
// already contains all 3 candidates' functions -- it has to, see above),
// AND -- via try_bind() (see demo_poly_base.lpc)
// -- that a swap really is registered as pending: replace_program()'s
// pending-swap bookkeeping is what make_lfun_funp() consults to refuse
// binding a closure, so a try_bind() failure here is direct evidence
// that replace_program_is_noop() correctly did NOT fire on this
// genuinely-transformative case.
//
// The FULL landed transformation -- exactly one candidate's behavior
// surviving, the other 2 candidates' AND the shell's own machinery
// genuinely gone -- is verified by forcing the deferred sweep
// synchronously, which requires calling remove_destructed_objects()
// directly at the C++ level; see
// ReplaceProgramPolymorphicShellDemoLandsForReal in src/tests/test_lpc.cc.
void do_tests() {
  object ob;

  // Force a specific concrete type via create()'s forced_species
  // argument -- exercising that deterministic-argument code path,
  // distinct from the random() path exercised below -- the way a real
  // spawn system's deterministic path (e.g. restoring a saved monster
  // type) would.
  ob = clone_object("/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_shell", "goblin");

  // Still running the shell's own program: it already contains every
  // candidate's functions (required -- replace_program() can only swap
  // to something already inherited)...
  ASSERT(function_exists("is_goblin", ob));
  ASSERT(function_exists("is_wolf", ob));
  ASSERT(function_exists("is_ghost", ob));
  // ...plus its own (about-to-be-shed) machinery, proving this genuinely
  // started as the shell and not already a bare concrete class.
  ASSERT(function_exists("shell_only_marker", ob));
  // A genuine swap is pending (the shell has 3 candidates' worth of
  // content beyond any single one), so binding must FAIL here -- this is
  // what would catch replace_program_is_noop() over-triggering on this
  // multi-candidate shell.
  ASSERT(ob->try_bind());

  destruct(ob);

  // Letting random() actually run end-to-end (no forced_species): every
  // outcome is one of the 3 known candidates, and the shell/candidate
  // split above holds identically regardless of which one gets picked.
  ob = clone_object("/clone/replace_program/demo_poly_shell");
  ASSERT(function_exists("is_goblin", ob));
  ASSERT(function_exists("is_wolf", ob));
  ASSERT(function_exists("is_ghost", ob));
  ASSERT(function_exists("shell_only_marker", ob));
  ASSERT(ob->try_bind());
  destruct(ob);
}
